>
  <h2>In your 70s</h2>
  <p>
    Most insurers still offer full-coverage plans in your 70s if your health answers allow it. This is often the last decade with a wide choice
    of insurers, so it's a good time to compare.
  </p>

  <h2>At 75</h2>
  <p>
    Prices climb faster from here, and some insurers lower the maximum coverage they'll offer. Locking in a price before another birthday can
    make a real difference.
  </p>

  <h2>At 80 and beyond</h2>
  <p>
    Many insurers accept new applicants up to about 85; a few stop sooner (limits vary by insurer and state). Smaller amounts, like $5,000 to
    $10,000, keep the monthly premium manageable. If health rules out other plans, guaranteed issue is still available up to its age limit.
    <a href="/burial-insurance-over-85/">What's available at 85 and older</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <h3>Buying for a parent?</h3>
    <p>
      Your parent needs to take part: they answer the health questions and sign the application. You can still be on the call to help, and you
      can often be the one who pays (the rules vary by insurer). <a href="/burial-insurance-for-parents/">How buying for a parent works</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Next steps</h2>
  <p>
    See <a href="/final-expense-insurance-cost/">sample prices by age</a>, or compare <a href="/guaranteed-issue-vs-simplified-issue/">plan types</a>.
  </p>
</Guide>
